Draw the **long ice floes**, a 2-frame **sprite sheet** of solid drifting ice rafts
for an arctic crossing game, using only the drawing tool.

These are the big solid floes the player rides across the open water — each a single
continuous slab of ice, NOT a row of separate pans. You are drawing two lengths on
the same 128x32 canvas (four 32-pixel tiles wide): a **three-tile** floe (frame 0,
filling the left three tiles) and a **four-tile** floe (frame 1, filling all four),
both on full transparency.

## How drawing works

The only way to make a mark is the `draw-sheet` binary already on your `PATH`. It is
the sole channel: you build the sheet by calling it **one operation at a time**, each
targeting a frame with `--frame <index>`. The ordered operations you issue — recorded
to a per-frame action log — are the **authoritative output that is scored**, not the
pixels on disk. An image produced any other way (writing a PNG directly, editing a
preview by hand) is discarded and flagged; only the recorded operations count.

Each frame is its **own separate 128×32 image**. After each call the binary
re-renders that frame to `frames/<index>.png` and appends the operation to that
frame's log. Blank starting frames and empty logs are already in place at
`{{workspace}}`.

Each operation is an ordinary subcommand with flags, naming its frame:

```
draw-sheet fill-rect --frame 1 --x 2 --y 6 --w 124 --h 22 --color "#c3dee9"
```

Run `draw-sheet --help` to list every operation and `draw-sheet <operation> --help`
for one operation's exact flags — that help text is the authoritative contract.
**Read `frames/<index>.png` after your calls** to see your progress and decide what
to do next.

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly.

## What to deliver

Build the floes up operation by operation until each `frames/<index>.png` matches the
brief: frame 0 a solid three-tile floe filling the left 96×32 (right tile
transparent), frame 1 a solid four-tile floe filling the full 128×32 — each one
continuous rounded slab (no internal seams) with a continuous snow top, ice edge, and
waterline, reading as a single long floe floating on water, on full transparency.
When you are satisfied, stop: the recorded per-frame action logs are your finished
submission.
